# Steps on any collection

Most steps take any collection: `map`, `filter`, `size`, `contains`, `sum`,
`min`, `reduce`. This page covers what they have in common, and how to move a
value from one collection type to another.

## A step returns the kind of collection it was given

`map` and `filter` over a list return a list, and over a set return a set:

```hql
[412, 280, 310, 145] | filter(n => n > 300)
```

```text
[412, 310]
```

```hql
{412, 280, 310, 145} | filter(n => n > 300)
```

```text
{412, 310}
```

The checker knows this too. The type of the result has the same constructor
as the input, so after `filter` a list is still a `List` and a set is still a
`Set`.

A step that produces an order returns a list, whatever it was given. `sort`
over a set returns a `List`, because the result has positions and a set
cannot hold them.

## Counting and asking

`size` is the number of elements. `count(x)` is the number of elements equal
to `x`, and `contains(x)` asks whether there is at least one:

```hql
genres = ["novel", "essay", "novel"]
[genres | size, genres | count("novel")]
```

```text
[3, 2]
```

```hql
["novel", "essay", "novel"] | contains("poetry")
```

```text
false
```

`count` without an argument is the same as `size`.

## Reducing to one value

`sum`, `product`, `average`, `min` and `max` reduce a collection of numbers
to one value. `average`, `min` and `max` return an `Option`, because an empty
collection has no average, least or greatest element:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | average
```

```text
334.0
```

`reduce` is the general form. It takes a starting value and a function that
combines the result so far with the next element:

```hql
[412, 280, 310] | reduce(0, (total, n) => total + n)
```

```text
1002
```

`sum` is `reduce(0, (a, b) => a + b)`, and `product` is
`reduce(1, (a, b) => a * b)`.

## Grouping

`count_by` counts the elements with each key, and `group` collects them. Both
take a function that returns the key of an element:

```hql
books = [
    {title: "Dune", genre: "sf"},
    {title: "Emma", genre: "novel"},
    {title: "Ubik", genre: "sf"},
]
books | count_by(b => b.genre)
```

```text
Map {"sf": 2, "novel": 1}
```

[[06-sql-like-queries|Chapter 6]] groups and joins collections the way SQL
groups and joins tables.

## Converting between collections

Every conversion between collection types is written in the program.

![Conversions between collection types: Set and sort move between List and Set, Map builds a map from two lists, zip pairs two lists, entries, keys and values take a map apart](conversions.svg)

| From | To | Write | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `List<T>` | `Set<T>` | `Set(xs)`, `xs \| Set` | repeated values are removed |
| `Set<T>` | `List<T>` | `xs \| sort` | the elements are put in order |
| `List<K>`, `List<V>` | `Map<K, V>` | `Map(keys, values)` | keys and values are paired by position |
| `List<K>`, `List<V>` | `List<Pair<K, V>>` | `zip(keys, values)` | the same pairing, as a list |
| `Map<K, V>` | `Set<K>` | `m.keys` | the keys |
| `Map<K, V>` | `List<V>` | `m \| values` | the values |
| `Map<K, V>` | `List<Pair<K, V>>` | `m \| entries` | each key with its value |

There is no `List(s)` for a set `s`. A list needs an order for its elements,
and a set does not have one, so the program must choose it:

```hql
{"sf", "novel"} | List
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: a List keeps positions, and a Set<String> has none; `sort` it into one
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | {"sf", "novel"} | List
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

```hql
{"sf", "novel"} | sort
```

```text
["novel", "sf"]
```
